{"name":"Black Box","hex":"#0f282f","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"black box","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A flight recorder; the brightly colored cockpit voice recorders and flight data recorders in an aircraft designed to aid in determining the cause of an accident.","Any similar device on motor vehicles, such as rail event recorders and ship trip recorders."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To focus on the inputs and outputs without worrying about the internal complexity."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/black_box"}],"entries":[{"title":"Black box (phreaking)","blocks":[{"text":"Black boxes were devices which, when attached to home phones, allowed all incoming calls to be received without charge to the caller."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_box_(phreaking)","wikidata":"Q2905331"},{"title":"Black box","blocks":[{"text":"In science, computing, and engineering, a black box is a system which can be viewed in terms of its inputs and outputs, without any knowledge of its internal workings. Its implementation is \"opaque\" (black). The term can be used to refer to many inner workings, such as those of a transistor, an engine, an algorithm, the human brain, or an institution or government. In regard to artificial intelligence, \"a 'black box' refers to an AI system whose internal workings are opaque or not understandable to users and observers.\""}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_box","wikidata":"Q29256"},{"title":"Box","blocks":[{"text":"A box is a container with rigid sides used for the storage or transportation of its contents. Most boxes have flat, parallel, rectangular sides. Boxes can be very small or very large and can be used for a variety of purposes, from functional to decorative."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/L%C3%A5da_-_Livrustkammaren_-_107142.tif/lossy-page1-330px-L%C3%A5da_-_Livrustkammaren_-_107142.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Box","wikidata":"Q188075"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Fuerzas_en_viraje.png/500px-Fuerzas_en_viraje.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Fuerzas_en_viraje.png/500px-Fuerzas_en_viraje.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ashby: even a simple bicycle is a black box. Mechanical forces can be observed, but not the interatomic forces holding it together.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFuerzas_en_viraje.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/MIT_Radiation_Laboratory_magnetron_tests_RL-35-193.jpg/500px-MIT_Radiation_Laboratory_magnetron_tests_RL-35-193.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/MIT_Radiation_Laboratory_magnetron_tests_RL-35-193.jpg/500px-MIT_Radiation_Laboratory_magnetron_tests_RL-35-193.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"Black boxes\" in the Radiation Lab","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMIT_Radiation_Laboratory_magnetron_tests_RL-35-193.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Hidrograma.png/500px-Hidrograma.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Hidrograma.png/500px-Hidrograma.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The observed hydrograph is a graphic of the response of a watershed (a blackbox) with its runoff (red) to an input of rainfall (blue).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHidrograma.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Blackbox3D-obs.